 "state_profile": {
  "voting_summary": "Pennsylvania offers no-excuse mail voting but forbids counties from touching mail ballots before 7 a.m. on election day, the root of its famous slow count. Polls close at 8 p.m. Eastern; election day precincts report first through the night while counties process mail in parallel, with Philadelphia and Allegheny mail batches landing overnight and into the following days. Election day voting ran R+15 against D+31 for mail, so Pennsylvania shows the classic red mirage: Republican-leaning early margins that erode as mail is counted over 24-48 hours.",
  "reporting_notes": "",
